cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: >-
  transfergap: bounding the simulation-to-field discrepancy from a handful of
  paired measurements, and inverting the bound into a measurement count
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-11-01
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  Bounds the discrepancy between a digital twin's prediction and a field
  measurement from the few paired points a test campaign actually delivers,
  and inverts each bound into the number of additional measurements that would
  bring it to a declared target at a declared confidence. Three families are
  provided at three different standings, with the standing printed in the same
  table as the number: distribution-free bounds (Hoeffding 1963, the
  Maurer-Pontil 2009 empirical Bernstein bound, Wilks' 1941 nonparametric
  one-sided tolerance limit and the exact normal-theory tolerance factor),
  which are conservative and explicit about it; resampling bounds (the
  nonparametric bootstrap and split conformal prediction), which are tighter
  and rest on exchangeability; and a learned gradient-boosted conditional
  quantile model with a conformalised repair. Exchangeability is the
  load-bearing assumption of the second family and is TESTED, not assumed, by
  a permutation test against the monotone-drift and step-change alternatives a
  flight-test card actually produces. Everything is scored on ACHIEVED
  COVERAGE at the declared confidence, never on tightness; width is reported
  in its own column and never enters the verdict. Validation is level 2.
  Measured over 80 independent synthetic campaigns, the raw learned
  conditional quantile under-covers its declared 0.95 at every field sample
  size -- 0.8761 at n=15, 0.8754 at 25, 0.8769 at 40 and 0.8843 at 60 -- while
  being the tightest limit at every one of them, 9.4 to 10.2 kN.m against 13.5
  to 18.3 for the limits that hold, with a per-campaign tolerance rate as low
  as 0.0500; the distribution-free bound is the only trustworthy answer at
  those sample sizes, and at n=15 and n=25 it is the only one that exists at
  all, since Wilks' limit needs 59 measurements and split conformal needs 19.
  Hoeffding's bound achieves coverage 1.0000 of the true mean over 300
  campaigns at every size, and its sample-count inversion reaches its target
  slack on 600 of 600 trials because the slack does not read the data, while
  the empirical Bernstein inversion -- a plug-in, hence a prediction -- reaches
  it 51.67 per cent of the time. The two distribution-free mean bounds cross at
  n=82, so the older and simpler 1963 inequality is the tighter one at every
  sample size a campaign delivers. The exponential bound is checked against
  the exact binomial tail over 72 grid points with no violations and a worst
  exact-to-bound ratio of 0.460412, and the conformal limit against the
  two-sided finite-sample coverage bracket of Lei et al. (2018) at five sample
  sizes with 40000 repetitions each. The exchangeability test has size never
  above 0.0767 against a declared 0.05 and power 0.4300 at n=15 rising to
  0.9967 at n=120, so a fifteen-point campaign cannot establish the assumption
  its tighter bounds need. Nineteen negative controls were run, anchored on
  the true mean, the true population quantile, the true conditional mean gap
  and the declared test size; 11 were declared must-fire and fired and 8 were
  declared must-not-fire and did not. All numbers are measured against
  synthetic paired records generated by the package's own declared equations;
  no measurement of any real aircraft, engine or test article appears
  anywhere. Research grade. Not flight-qualified, not certified, not approved
  for operational aerospace use.
authors:
  - name: "OPTIMA Organisation"
keywords:
  - digital-twin
  - simulation-to-field
  - concentration-inequality
  - empirical-bernstein
  - tolerance-interval
  - conformal-prediction
  - exchangeability
  - sample-size
references:
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Hoeffding
        given-names: "W."
    title: "Probability inequalities for sums of bounded random variables"
    journal: "Journal of the American Statistical Association"
    volume: 58
    issue: 301
    year: 1963
    start: 13
    end: 30
    notes: >-
      Theorem 2 is equation (2) of transfergap.bounds, and equation (3) is its
      one-sided inversion at a declared confidence. The closed-form sample
      count of equation (10) follows because the slack does not depend on the
      data.
  - type: conference-paper
    authors:
      - family-names: Maurer
        given-names: "A."
      - family-names: Pontil
        given-names: "M."
    title: "Empirical Bernstein bounds and sample-variance penalization"
    collection-title: >-
      Proceedings of the 22nd Conference on Learning Theory (COLT 2009)
    year: 2009
    notes: >-
      Theorem 4 is equation (5) of transfergap.bounds, rescaled from [0, 1] to
      [0, R] by positive homogeneity. Its measured crossover with Hoeffding's
      bound on this package's synthetic population is at n = 82.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Wilks
        given-names: "S. S."
    title: "Determination of sample sizes for setting tolerance limits"
    journal: "Annals of Mathematical Statistics"
    volume: 12
    issue: 1
    year: 1941
    start: 91
    end: 96
    notes: >-
      The Beta(n, 1) law of the coverage of the largest order statistic, and
      hence the exact closed-form requirement n >= ln(delta)/ln(p) of equation
      (7). Verified independently here against scipy.stats.beta over 40000
      repetitions.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Wald
        given-names: "A."
    title: "An extension of Wilks' method for setting tolerance limits"
    journal: "Annals of Mathematical Statistics"
    volume: 14
    issue: 1
    year: 1943
    start: 45
    end: 55
    notes: >-
      The extension to several order statistics and to multivariate tolerance
      regions. This package uses only the largest order statistic, where the
      inversion is closed form, and cites this for the general case it does
      not implement.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Krishnamoorthy
        given-names: "K."
      - family-names: Mathew
        given-names: "T."
    title: "Statistical Tolerance Regions: Theory, Applications, and Computation"
    publisher:
      name: "Wiley"
    year: 2009
    notes: >-
      Chapter 1, the exact one-sided normal tolerance factor from the
      non-central t distribution, equation (9). Four of its tabulated values
      are known-answer tests here; the worst measured deviation is 6.81e-4.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Efron
        given-names: "B."
      - family-names: Tibshirani
        given-names: "R. J."
    title: "An Introduction to the Bootstrap"
    publisher:
      name: "Chapman and Hall"
    year: 1993
    notes: >-
      Chapters 12 and 13, the percentile and basic bootstrap confidence
      intervals, equations (14) and (15). Both are marked not
      distribution-free here and both measurably under-cover at the sample
      sizes measured.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Vovk
        given-names: "V."
      - family-names: Gammerman
        given-names: "A."
      - family-names: Shafer
        given-names: "G."
    title: "Algorithmic Learning in a Random World"
    publisher:
      name: "Springer"
    year: 2005
    notes: >-
      The conformal prediction framework and the exchangeability argument that
      gives equation (12) its finite-sample validity.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Angelopoulos
        given-names: "A. N."
      - family-names: Bates
        given-names: "S."
    title: "Conformal prediction: a gentle introduction"
    journal: "Foundations and Trends in Machine Learning"
    volume: 16
    issue: 4
    year: 2023
    start: 494
    end: 591
    notes: >-
      Section 1, the modern statement of the split-conformal quantile
      k = ceil((n+1)(1-alpha)) used as equation (12).
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Lei
        given-names: "J."
      - family-names: "G'Sell"
        given-names: "M."
      - family-names: Rinaldo
        given-names: "A."
      - family-names: Tibshirani
        given-names: "R. J."
      - family-names: Wasserman
        given-names: "L."
    title: "Distribution-free predictive inference for regression"
    journal: "Journal of the American Statistical Association"
    volume: 113
    issue: 523
    year: 2018
    start: 1094
    end: 1111
    notes: >-
      Theorem 2.2, the upper half of the coverage bracket of equation (13).
      The bracket is two-sided and is used here as a bound derived from
      mathematics the implementation does not use, since the implementation
      takes an order statistic by integer index and computes no coverage
      probability.
  - type: conference-paper
    authors:
      - family-names: Romano
        given-names: "Y."
      - family-names: Patterson
        given-names: "E."
      - family-names: "Candes"
        given-names: "E. J."
    title: "Conformalized quantile regression"
    collection-title: "Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems"
    volume: 32
    year: 2019
    notes: >-
      The conformal repair of a fitted conditional quantile. Measured here to
      restore coverage from 0.8769 to 0.9571 at n = 40 at the cost of 5.1 kN.m
      of width, and to require more data than the model it repairs.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Koenker
        given-names: "R."
      - family-names: Bassett
        given-names: "G."
