    title: "Testing Statistical Hypotheses"
    edition: "3rd"
    publisher:
      name: "Springer"
    year: 2005
    notes: >-
      Chapter 15, permutation and randomisation tests. Under exchangeability
      the labels are exchangeable by definition, so the permutation null of
      equations (16) and (17) is exact in finite samples.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Phipson
        given-names: "B."
      - family-names: Smyth
        given-names: "G. K."
    title: >-
      Permutation p-values should never be zero: calculating exact p-values
      when permutations are randomly drawn
    journal: "Statistical Applications in Genetics and Molecular Biology"
    volume: 9
    issue: 1
    year: 2010
    notes: >-
      Article 39. The +1 in both numerator and denominator of equation (18),
      which makes the Monte Carlo p-value valid for every number of
      permutations. The naive ratio can return zero, which claims more than
      any finite number of permutations supports.
  - type: article
    authors:
